WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.470
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Manufacturing
creates a lot more

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:02.640
than just physical goods.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.980
It also creates a
tremendous amount of data.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.750
Join us as we talk
to Oden technologies

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.180
about how they built a
system to help manufacturers

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.450
analyze their data using IoT on
this episode of "Stack Chat."

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.340
John, thanks so much
for joining us today.

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.940
Tell us a little bit more about
what Oden Technologies does.

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:23.190
JOHN TUREK: Mark, thanks
for hosting me today Oden

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.820
is a company that focuses
on the internet of things

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.270
for the industrial sector
with the specific mission

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:34.540
of improving the efficiency
of the manufacturing process.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.140
For example, we
have customers that

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:45.000
produce $500 million to $1
billion worth of product a year

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.050
with high scrap rates.

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:48.870
And by leveraging
the Oden platform,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.890
they're able to reduce, as one
factor, the amount of scrap

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.750
material that they create.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.250
In one case, we've
had a customer

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.980
that has managed to
reduce their scrap

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.988
levels from 23% down to 9% after
implementing the Oden solution.

00:01:03.988 --> 00:01:05.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That's great.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.800
So it kind of captures
a lot of data,

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.600
and then can somehow relay
that back to the customers.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.480
What does that actual
architecture look like?

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.580
JOHN TUREK: A big part
of our architecture

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.830
is around managing the data that
we've collected from the users

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.850
and making that data
available and actionable.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.680
The starting point
for all of this

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.000
is, of course, the
collection of data.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.580
We start with a device that
sits on the factory floor, which

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:36.060
collects data through the PLCs.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.640
That data then gets sent up
to the cloud using Google IoT.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.940
We pull the data off of
Google IoT using Pub/Sub.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.940
We process that
data in real-time.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.100
We run whatever learning models
we have against the data.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.120
We also move the data
over into a time series

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.360
database that sits
on top of Bigtable,

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.940
as well as into BigQuery.

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:04.620
In addition to collecting
data from the machinery,

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.810
we also collect data
from other sources,

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:09.509
for example, the operators
through an operator interface

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:11.760
that we have on
the factory floor.

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:14.520
If a machine is down or
there's a production problem,

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.900
the operator will be able to
add data or add annotations

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:21.630
that allows us to then go
back and do further analysis.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:24.820
And that comes back in
through the cloud as well.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.950
Much of that data then gets
stored into Google SQL.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:32.913
And we can then pull that data
out as well using BigQuery.

00:02:32.913 --> 00:02:34.830
MARK MIRCHANDANI: If you
could kind of go back

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.830
to when you started,
were there any decisions

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.443
that you think you might change?

00:02:39.443 --> 00:02:41.610
JOHN TUREK: In hindsight,
there are always decisions

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:43.650
that we would make differently.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.120
One example is our
choice of to use a graph

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.700
database to represent
the entities that

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.250
sit on the factory floor.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.160
At the time that the
decision was made,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.490
the observation was that
the factory floor does not

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.622
follow a strictly
hierarchical model

00:03:01.622 --> 00:03:04.080
and the way that you want to
view data on the factory floor

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.290
does not follow a strictly
hierarchical model.

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.810
You will sometimes want
to organize your data

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.835
by factory line.

00:03:11.835 --> 00:03:13.710
You will sometimes want
to organize your data

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:17.160
by, for example,
type of machine.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.100
We decided to use a graph
database at the time.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.160
We thought that it would
accelerate our development

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.710
efforts, and it did.

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.080
However, as we
have scaled up, we

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.040
have started to run into the
limitations of that graph,

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:32.560
of the graph database.

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.270
One of the areas of
concern is the fact

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.850
that graph databases
tend to follow--

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:40.290
be schema-less.

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.480
And so what we have found
is that sometimes engineers

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.750
will introduce new features.

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:50.940
And because there isn't
a lot of structure,

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.430
by introducing new
features, assumptions

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:56.340
that are inherent in the
scheme-less architecture

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:01.660
end up creating
issues down the road.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:05.310
A second choice that we made
was to use an abstraction layer

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:09.210
on top of Bigtable to support
our time series database.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:12.280
The choice of Bigtable
has been great.

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.860
Bigtable scales wonderfully.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:19.440
And more recently, Google has
added replication capabilities.

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:26.990
That has been highly beneficial
to our redundancy strategy.

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:30.160
So we feel that we're
much more reliable.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.610
However, at the
time, we had a choice

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.440
between a few different
abstraction layers.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:37.020
And the abstraction
layer that we chose

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.750
has reached somewhat of a dead
end in the public community,

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:43.710
which means that it is not being
actively developed or worked

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.310
on by others.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.820
And so features that
were missing at the time

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.920
remain missing, while
other abstraction layers

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:52.090
have moved forward.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:56.720
If we were to start again today,
we would probably use TSDB.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:59.820
And I fully expect that over
the next 12 to 18 months,

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:03.360
we will be making the move to
using TSDB as our abstraction

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:03.960
layer.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.460
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Thanks so much
for coming in and telling us

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:08.328
about how you kind of built
this architecture to scale.

00:05:08.328 --> 00:05:10.120
JOHN TUREK: Thank you
for hosting me today.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.198
And I really enjoyed our chat.

00:05:12.198 --> 00:05:14.490
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Try setting
up your own data pipeline

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.823
by using this hands-on solution
that walks through using

00:05:16.823 --> 00:05:18.726
Dataflow and BigQuery.

